    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The assignment was to design and build a responsive blog using content managed through the
        Noroff API. Visitors needed to browse and read posts without signing in. An authenticated
        administrator also needed a practical way to create, edit, and delete content. I developed
        the visual identity alongside the interface so the finished site would feel like a specific
        dog-agility publication rather than a general blog template.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The public blog and the private publishing workflow depend on the same post data, but
            they serve very different tasks. The interface had to expose editing controls only to an
            authenticated administrator while keeping the reading experience straightforward for
            everyone else. Because titles, images, excerpts, and article lengths come from the API,
            the layout also needed to remain usable when the content changed.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Separate public reading from actions that require authentication</li>
            <li>Render changing API content without making the layout fragile</li>
            <li>Preserve the brand’s playful character while keeping articles easy to read</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--homepage'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-src='site-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits desktop blog homepage showing branded navigation and dynamically loaded posts'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Branded homepage with dynamic blog content</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I separated API requests, authentication, post rendering, and form handling into
              focused JavaScript modules. This kept data access away from the presentation logic and
              made shared behaviour easier to trace.
            </li>
            <li>
              Posts remain publicly available, while create, edit, and delete controls depend on the
              administrator’s authentication state. Protected actions are not presented as part of
              the ordinary reading interface.
            </li>
            <li>
              The post cards and article layouts allow for different title lengths, image
              proportions, and amounts of body content. The design responds to the data instead of
              assuming every post will match the original mock-up.
            </li>
            <li>
              I built the identity around bold colour, typography, and custom dog-agility graphics.
              Navigation and form controls stay familiar, giving the expressive parts of the design
              room without making basic actions harder to recognise.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--admin'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-agilitybanditsmobile-600.webp'
            data-src='site-agilitybanditsmobile-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='488'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits mobile homepage and authenticated edit-post interface'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Mobile reading and edit-post views</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        Agility Bandits combines a responsive public blog with an authenticated content-management
        flow. Building it strengthened my understanding of API requests, persistent authentication,
        conditional interface states, form handling, and rendering content that cannot be predicted
        in advance. It also brought my design and development experience together in a useful way.
        The brand creates the personality, but the structure still has to work when an administrator
        publishes a new post with completely different content.
      </p>
    </div>

